From 08228372d9f212f359b7c63fae4d130cbcae2c66 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Farhan Riaz <its-forum@techlogix.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 03:21:39 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 04/11] core: Release the libraries of unloaded movies

A movie's library was kept in a map weakly keyed on the movie, on the
assumption that the library would go away with the movie. It cannot: the
library holds a strong `Arc<SwfMovie>` of its own, and so does nearly
every character in it, so the key's strong count never reaches zero. Once
a SWF had been loaded, its characters, decoded bitmaps and SWF data
stayed resident for the rest of the session, whether or not anything
could still reach them. Content that loads and unloads assets - a game
changing zone, or swapping equipment - grew without bound.

Three things kept that memory alive, and all three had to go:

* The library now records, weakly, the display object its movie was
  loaded into. Once that object has been collected, no timeline and no
  script can ask the library for a character any more, so the library is
  dropped and its movie with it. Movies without such a content root - the
  root movie above all - are never swept.

* `Avm2ClassRegistry` traced its keys, which made every class named by a
  `SymbolClass` tag a GC root. A class owns its translation unit, which
  owns the movie, which was the entry's own weak value, so the entry
  could never expire and the SWF behind it was pinned along with its
  scripts and their globals. Classes are now held weakly and dead entries
  are swept.

* The collector paced itself against the size of its own allocations
  only. A movie's characters are small objects pointing at very large
  buffers, so a session could hold hundreds of megabytes of dead content
  while the collector believed it had almost nothing to do. Libraries now
  report the SWF data and bitmap sources they keep resident.

`Loader.unloadAndStop` stops being a stub while we are here, since it is
what content calls to say it is finished with an asset: it now stops the
sounds and timelines of the content it is discarding, and its `gc`
argument charges the collector so the memory comes back promptly instead
of whenever the collector next has reason to run.

Adds a test that loads and unloads the same SWF ten times over and checks
that what stays resident does not grow with the number of loads.
